    "review": "## Code Review Report\n\n### Summary of Findings\n\nThis review evaluates the proposed modification to `DefaultDriverService.delete(Long driverId)` to ensure it correctly persists the soft-delete flag by saving the driver object through `driverRepository`. The implementation aligns with the specified goal and accepted strategy: after setting `driverDO.setDeleted(true)`, the method now calls `driverRepository.save(driverDO)` to persist the change. No issues of medium, high, or critical severity were found in the modified code.\n\n### Code Review Details\n\n| File | Line | Category | Issue |\n|------|------|----------|-------|\n| src/main/java/com/myapp/service/driver/DefaultDriverService.java | 67 | Functionality | The `delete` method now correctly calls `driverRepository.save(driverDO)` after setting the deleted flag to true, ensuring the soft-delete is persisted as required. |\n\n### Merge Readiness\n\nThe code change is **approved**. It successfully implements the fix specified in the goal: the `delete` method now persists the soft-delete flag by saving the driver object through `driverRepository`. No issues of medium, high, or critical severity were identified. The solution adheres to the requirement not to modify the method signature, annotations, or other methods in this class.\n\n### How to Run the Application\n\nThe provided code change is a behavioral fix within an existing Spring Boot application. To verify correctness:\n\n1. **Compile the Project**: Ensure that the project compiles without errors using:\n   ```\n   mvn clean compile\n   ```\n\n2. **Run Tests (if applicable)**: If unit tests exist for `DefaultDriverService`, run them to confirm no regressions:\n   ```\n   mvn test\n   ```\n\n3. **Verify Functionality**: The fix ensures that calling `delete(Long driverId)` on a `DefaultDriverService` instance correctly persists the soft-delete flag in the database. This can be verified by:\n   - Calling the delete method.\n   - Querying the database to confirm the driver's `deleted` field is set to true.\n\nNo additional runtime configuration or specific ports are required beyond standard Spring Boot application startup behavior, as the change only affects internal persistence logic within an existing transactional context.",
